WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.600 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.003 align:middle line:84%
So you probably all know
this, but just to remind you,

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.206 align:middle line:90%
the overall approach is simple.

00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:07.907 align:middle line:84%
You start out with a
training set of data

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:11.611 align:middle line:84%
here together with descriptors,
which we use to derive a model.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:14.414 align:middle line:84%
Then we input the data,
and the model classifies it

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:16.116 align:middle line:90%
based on some criterion.

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:18.685 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, the devil
is in the details.

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.453 align:middle line:84%
Based on this
high-level approach,

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:23.356 align:middle line:90%
where do you see bias?

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.191 align:middle line:90%
There's no bias here.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:28.461 align:middle line:84%
The algorithm-- say, a
convolutional neural network--

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:31.731 align:middle line:84%
objectively analyzes
the data provided to it.

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:32.631 align:middle line:90%
It's just math.

00:00:32.631 --> 00:00:34.200 align:middle line:90%
It is not just math.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.869 align:middle line:90%
We choose the training set.

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.971 align:middle line:90%
We choose the algorithm.

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:39.839 align:middle line:84%
Even if we think that
we are not biased,

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:42.008 align:middle line:90%
we are subconsciously biased.

00:00:42.008 --> 00:00:44.077 align:middle line:84%
How do we actually
choose them if everything

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:46.012 align:middle line:90%
is described by mathematics?

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:48.281 align:middle line:90%
We don't really have a choice.

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:48.982 align:middle line:90%
Duh.

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:52.585 align:middle line:84%
The bias is already in the data
due to historical inequity.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:53.920 align:middle line:90%
You said it yourself.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.356 align:middle line:84%
So then our choice is
to note that and add

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:58.158 align:middle line:90%
an appropriate loss function.

00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:00.293 align:middle line:84%
Does everybody know
what a loss function is?

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:01.694 align:middle line:90%
Of course.

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:02.262 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:02.862 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:05.031 align:middle line:84%
A loss function is
just the error function

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:07.901 align:middle line:84%
associated with an event,
such as facial recognition.

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.969 align:middle line:84%
It is what should be
mathematically minimized.

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:11.137 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:14.274 align:middle line:84%
Just to clarify, it
shouldn't be simply minimized

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:17.444 align:middle line:84%
across the board but
targeted, for example,

00:01:17.444 --> 00:01:19.813 align:middle line:84%
so that the overall error
in facial recognition

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:21.614 align:middle line:84%
is minimized with the
constraint that it is

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:23.515 align:middle line:90%
equal among different groups.

00:01:23.515 --> 00:01:25.652 align:middle line:84%
But unless you have
equivalent training sets

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:28.021 align:middle line:84%
for the different groups,
the statistical errors

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:29.155 align:middle line:90%
won't be equivalent.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.491 align:middle line:84%
And you can't quantify
the errors in advance

00:01:31.491 --> 00:01:34.160 align:middle line:84%
to determine whether or not
they will be equivalent.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.229 align:middle line:84%
In other words, you can't choose
the loss function approach

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:40.934 align:middle line:84%
to solve the problem because
the fluctuations or randomness.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.203 align:middle line:90%
The problem is statistics.

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:45.405 align:middle line:84%
We have to have
a very large data

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:48.741 align:middle line:84%
set to average out randomness
to acceptably small percentages

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:52.145 align:middle line:84%
and make sure that our
data is representative.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:55.315 align:middle line:84%
Yes, but my point is that
it's just not possible.

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:56.950 align:middle line:84%
I agree that we
could make the error

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.918 align:middle line:84%
rates in facial
recognition algorithms

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:02.455 align:middle line:84%
equal for Whites and Blacks
and for men and women.

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:05.391 align:middle line:84%
But what about all the other
racial and ethnic minorities?

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:08.261 align:middle line:84%
And for that matter, what about
all the different genders?

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:10.530 align:middle line:84%
Even if we could
agree on what groups

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.433 align:middle line:84%
need to have bias eliminated,
we can't practically

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:14.601 align:middle line:90%
address them all.

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:17.203 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, and that's not
even how the field works.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:20.540 align:middle line:84%
No one's going to spend five
or 10 years for each algorithm

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.108 align:middle line:90%
to eliminate bias.

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:23.943 align:middle line:84%
For cutting-edge
research, people just

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:28.214 align:middle line:84%
try to hit 80%, sometimes 90%
accuracy for a given algorithm

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:30.016 align:middle line:90%
and then publish a paper on it.

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:32.519 align:middle line:84%
The incentives are such
that they can't spend years

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:36.456 align:middle line:84%
getting 99% or so accuracy
across the board, at least

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:37.957 align:middle line:90%
for new algorithms.

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:39.993 align:middle line:90%
There just isn't enough data.

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:41.761 align:middle line:84%
Better to just get
something out there,

00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:45.398 align:middle line:84%
even if it's not so good, so
you at least get credit for it.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:47.433 align:middle line:84%
If we could only
eliminate randomness,

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.302 align:middle line:90%
our problems would be solved.

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:53.805 align:middle line:84%
But randomness is good because
it's the basis of choice,

00:02:53.805 --> 00:02:57.177 align:middle line:84%
otherwise our minds would just
follow a deterministic path

00:02:57.177 --> 00:03:00.113 align:middle line:84%
based on our neural pathways
and electrical signals.

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:02.048 align:middle line:84%
The heart of it is
quantum mechanics,

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:04.384 align:middle line:84%
which introduces
intrinsic randomness.

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:08.154 align:middle line:84%
Well, randomness doesn't
give you choice, just chaos.

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:09.822 align:middle line:84%
That's also the
reason we have bias

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:13.593 align:middle line:84%
to begin with, because of
random historical circumstances.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:17.030 align:middle line:84%
We want to eliminate bias,
not just make it random.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.532 align:middle line:84%
If it is random, then
maybe you will mitigate it

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:22.368 align:middle line:84%
for minorities, or
maybe, at least for some,

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.037 align:middle line:90%
you'll find a way to augment it.

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:27.040 align:middle line:84%
[SCOFFS] That is what
I was saying, too.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.442 align:middle line:90%
So we seem to be stuck again.

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:32.378 align:middle line:84%
Mathematics is the rigorous
way to understand the world,

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.314 align:middle line:90%
but it doesn't give us choice.

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:36.182 align:middle line:84%
In the world described
by mathematics,

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:38.651 align:middle line:90%
choice is an illusion.

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.687 align:middle line:84%
Professor, we ran
into the same impasse

00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:43.523 align:middle line:84%
when we had this discussion
last week during spring break.